--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0730 established that loading a bootstrapped add-on's `bootstrap.js` a
second time into a scope that has already evaluated it throws at parse time,
because `var` and `function` tolerate redeclaration while `const`, `let` and
`class` do not. PR #444 converted `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` and added a
regression arm — **for that file only**.

The sweep, run over every `bootstrap.js` this tree ships:

    plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js                          0   (fixed, #444)
    bench/zotero-fulltext-plugin/bootstrap.js               12
    verification/probes/sdt-diagnostic-plugin/bootstrap.js    4
    verification/probes/sdt-sitter-harness/bootstrap.js       1
    verification/probes/sdt-diagnostic-harness/bootstrap.js   0
    verification/probes/sdt-phase-harness/bootstrap.js        0

`bench/zotero-fulltext-plugin` is not hypothetical: it is
`fulltext-control@search-works-for-zotero.invalid`, and on 2026-09-08 it was
the one other add-on installed and `active: true` in the author's profile.

## What is and is not established

**Latent, not live.** Ticket 0727's arm 3, run the same morning, installed a
build carrying the un-converted bootstrap over a live one and it started
cleanly — dialog, pulse, census, cache write. So Zotero hands a replaced add-on
a FRESH scope, and on the upgrade path this defect cannot fire.

**But 0730's own question was never about the upgrade path.** It asked about
DISABLE/RE-ENABLE — "turn the add-on off, then on again", the recovery the
sitter's own UI recommends and which the launch-declined phase forces on the
user. That is a different Gecko path, nothing has measured it, and it is one
toggle away from being measured.

So this ticket is a guard, not a repair. The failure mode it guards against is
the one worth guarding: a parse error takes the whole file, `startup()` never
runs, no journal record is written, and the window keeps whatever the previous
instance left on screen. Silent and total, and indistinguishable from the stale
button that cost ticket 0688 several weeks.

## What this ticket asks for

A standing test that loads EVERY `bootstrap.js` this tree ships twice into one
`vm` context and asserts the second load succeeds — discovered by walking the
tree, not by a hand-written list. A hand-listed guard covers the files someone
remembered; the next plugin added is exactly the one it will not cover, which is
the failure `feedback_a_move_can_leave_the_gate` names.

`tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` already carries the single-file arm from #444
and is the model: create a context, run the source, run it again, assert no
throw. The generalisation is the discovery step, not the assertion.

## Relevant files

- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` — the existing one-file arm to generalise
- `bench/zotero-fulltext-plugin/bootstrap.js` — 12 bindings, live-installed
- `verification/probes/sdt-diagnostic-plugin/bootstrap.js` — 4
- `verification/probes/sdt-sitter-harness/bootstrap.js` — 1

## Actions

1. Write the class guard: discover every `bootstrap.js` under the plugin and
   probe directories, load each twice into a fresh context, assert the second
   load does not throw. It must FAIL today against the three files above —
   that is the control, and it must be seen red before anything is converted.
2. Convert the three files' top-level `const`/`let` to `var`, for the same two
   reasons the sitter's carry: redeclaration tolerance, and the sandbox
   exposure ticket 0695 recorded.
3. Leave a comment in each converted file saying why, so a future reader
   tidying them back to `const` meets the reason rather than a style choice.

## Test

The guard IS the test, and its control is already available: it must redden
against the tree as it stands (three files fail), and green after the
conversion. Run it red first — a guard whose red state nobody has seen is a
guard nobody can trust, and this tree has been bitten by exactly that
(0693's vacuous privacy test, demonstrated by restoring the defect).

Second control, cheaper and worth having: point the discovery step at a
directory with no `bootstrap.js` and confirm it reports NOT-RUN rather than
passing over an empty set. A guard that greens because it found nothing to
check is the failure this repository keeps meeting.

## Verification

- [x] The guard reddens against the current tree, naming all three files
      — recorded in this log's 2026-09-08T09:57Z entry: red before any
      conversion, naming exactly the three files and neither the already-
      converted sitter nor the two zero-binding harnesses.
- [x] It greens after the conversion — `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`
      "every bootstrap.js this tree ships loads twice into one scope without
      throwing" passes; all four files now carry zero top-level
      `const`/`let`/`class`, and each converted file names tickets 0730/0741
      in a comment saying why (action 3).
- [x] It discovers files by walking the tree, and a newly added plugin is
      covered without editing the test — `discoverBootstraps` at
      `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs:1041` recurses from the repository root,
      refusing only dot-directories and the named unshipped checkouts.
- [x] An empty discovery set reports NOT-RUN, not a pass —
      `doubleLoadReport` returns a three-valued verdict (:1077) and the
      assertion at :1082 refuses NOT-RUN; the control at :1096 is two-sided,
      proving both that an empty tree reports NOT-RUN and that a planted
      broken file reaches FAIL, plus that both skip-list shapes actually skip.
- [x] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Ticket 0695's `var` bindings stay `var`, in every file.
- The guard does not import or execute plugin behaviour beyond the double
  load: it is a parse-level assertion, and widening it into a behavioural test
  of four plugins is how it becomes slow and gets deleted.

## Exit criteria

Every `bootstrap.js` in the tree survives a double load, the guard finds them
by walking rather than by a list, and its red state has been seen.
